1
00:00:02,711 --> 00:00:05,088
Okay, Babydoll Pink, let's see
if you can cover up the fact

2
00:00:05,130 --> 00:00:07,382
that I got my dad's feet.

3
00:00:07,424 --> 00:00:09,134
LEONARD:
It's just two
degrees, Sheldon.

4
00:00:09,176 --> 00:00:11,846
I just want to turn up the
thermostat two degrees!

5
00:00:11,887 --> 00:00:14,807
SHELDON:
Let me point out that two
degrees can be the difference

6
00:00:14,849 --> 00:00:16,308
between water and steam.

7
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:18,644
LEONARD:
Yes, if we lived in
a teakettle.

8
00:00:18,686 --> 00:00:20,145
SHELDON:
This is the temperature
you agreed to

9
00:00:20,187 --> 00:00:21,689
in the roommate agreement.

10
00:00:21,730 --> 00:00:23,732
LEONARD: Aw, screw
the roommate agreement!

11
00:00:23,774 --> 00:00:25,776
SHELDON:
No, you don't screw
the roommate agreement.

12
00:00:25,818 --> 00:00:28,487
The roommate agreement
screws you.

13
00:00:28,529 --> 00:00:30,906
LEONARD:
You know what, go to Hell
and set their thermostat.

14
00:00:30,948 --> 00:00:32,491
SHELDON:
I don't have
to go to Hell.

15
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:36,328
At 73 degrees,
I'm there already!

16
00:00:36,370 --> 00:00:37,872
(door slams)

17
00:00:37,913 --> 00:00:42,041
(insistent knocking)
Who is it?

18
00:00:42,083 --> 00:00:43,293
Leonard.

19
00:00:43,335 --> 00:00:45,128
Hang on.

20
00:00:50,009 --> 00:00:51,927
Can I sleep on
your couch tonight?

21
00:00:51,969 --> 00:00:53,595
Uh, well, you can try,

22
00:00:53,637 --> 00:00:55,764
but the people across the hall
are being very noisy.

23
00:00:55,806 --> 00:00:58,099
You heard that, huh?

24
00:00:58,141 --> 00:01:01,186
Apparently, the one fella tried
to adjust the thermostat,

25
00:01:01,228 --> 00:01:03,856
then the other fella
went bat-crap crazy.

26
00:01:03,898 --> 00:01:06,274
So you agree, he's nuts.

27
00:01:06,316 --> 00:01:08,736
Well, not as nuts as the guy
who chooses to live with him.

28
00:01:08,777 --> 00:01:10,821
Believe it or not, he was worse
when I met him.

29
00:01:10,863 --> 00:01:13,240
Oh, I do not believe that.

30
00:01:13,281 --> 00:01:15,659
You are so naive.

31
00:01:15,701 --> 00:01:17,578
Just like I was
seven years ago.

32
00:01:19,204 --> 00:01:21,749
I'd just started
at the university.

33
00:01:26,587 --> 00:01:28,213
Excuse me, I'm looking

34
00:01:28,255 --> 00:01:30,632
for Sheldon Cooper's apartment.

35
00:01:30,674 --> 00:01:32,593
Oh, I bet you're here
to check out the room for rent.

36
00:01:32,634 --> 00:01:34,845
Yeah.
Run away, dude.

37
00:01:36,388 --> 00:01:37,723
What?!

38
00:01:37,765 --> 00:01:41,060
Run fast, run far.

39
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:43,437
That should have been
my first clue.

40
00:01:45,230 --> 00:01:48,692
♪ Our whole universe
was in a hot, dense state ♪

41
00:01:48,734 --> 00:01:51,695
♪ Then nearly 14 billion years
ago expansion started... Wait! ♪

42
00:01:51,737 --> 00:01:53,655
♪ The Earth began to cool ♪

43
00:01:53,697 --> 00:01:56,533
♪ The autotrophs began to drool,
Neanderthals developed tools ♪

44
00:01:56,575 --> 00:01:58,911
♪ We built the Wall ♪
♪ We built the pyramids ♪

45
00:01:58,953 --> 00:02:01,622
♪ Math, Science, History,
unraveling the mystery ♪

46
00:02:01,663 --> 00:02:03,749
♪ That all started
with a big bang ♪

47
00:02:03,791 --> 00:02:05,333
♪ Bang! ♪

48
00:02:10,213 --> 00:02:12,507
So Sheldon's last roommate
tried to warn you off?

49
00:02:12,549 --> 00:02:15,970
For all I knew,
he was the crazy one.

50
00:02:16,011 --> 00:02:18,221
He had this really
deranged look.

51
00:02:18,263 --> 00:02:21,349
Well, yeah, he'd been living
with Sheldon.

52
00:02:21,391 --> 00:02:24,436
Sure, it makes sense now.

53
00:02:27,147 --> 00:02:29,691
Anyway, I went upstairs
and knocked on the door.

54
00:02:35,656 --> 00:02:37,156
(deep voice):
Yeah?

55
00:02:46,083 --> 00:02:48,002
Dr. Cooper?

56
00:02:48,043 --> 00:02:51,170
No, you want the crazy
guy across the hall.

57
00:02:55,050 --> 00:02:58,219
In retrospect,
that was clue number two.

58
00:03:04,601 --> 00:03:05,853
Yes?

59
00:03:05,894 --> 00:03:07,437
I'm Leonard Hofstadter.

60
00:03:07,479 --> 00:03:09,230
I called you about
the apartment. You said...

61
00:03:09,272 --> 00:03:10,231
I know what I said.

62
00:03:10,273 --> 00:03:11,733
I know what you said.

63
00:03:11,775 --> 00:03:14,153
I know what my mother said
on March 5, 1992.

64
00:03:15,278 --> 00:03:17,614
What is the sixth noble gas?

65
00:03:17,656 --> 00:03:20,159
What?
You said you're
a scientist.

66
00:03:20,199 --> 00:03:21,827
What is the sixth noble gas?

67
00:03:21,869 --> 00:03:23,078
Uh, radon?

68
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:26,289
Are you asking me
or telling me?

69
00:03:26,331 --> 00:03:28,125
Telling you?

70
00:03:30,251 --> 00:03:33,088
Telling you.

71
00:03:33,130 --> 00:03:35,340
All right, next question.

72
00:03:35,382 --> 00:03:37,092
Kirk or Picard?

73
00:03:37,134 --> 00:03:39,720
Oh, uh, well, that's tricky.

74
00:03:39,761 --> 00:03:43,140
Um, Original Series
over Next Generation,

75
00:03:43,182 --> 00:03:44,975
but Picard over Kirk.

76
00:03:45,017 --> 00:03:46,267
Correct.

77
00:03:48,062 --> 00:03:50,147
You've passed the first
barrier to roommate-hood.

78
00:03:51,023 --> 00:03:52,524
You may enter.

79
00:03:52,566 --> 00:03:54,902
Oh, this is pretty nice.

80
00:04:02,159 --> 00:04:04,369
Uh, the bedrooms are back there?

81
00:04:04,411 --> 00:04:05,579
That depends.

82
00:04:05,620 --> 00:04:06,621
I don't understand--

83
00:04:06,663 --> 00:04:09,290
their existence is conditional?

84
00:04:09,332 --> 00:04:12,627
No, but your ability to perceive
their existence is conditional

85
00:04:12,669 --> 00:04:15,214
on you passing the second
and third barriers.

86
00:04:15,255 --> 00:04:16,840
There's three?

87
00:04:16,882 --> 00:04:19,009
Each more daunting
than the last.

88
00:04:20,552 --> 00:04:21,428
Have a seat.

89
00:04:21,469 --> 00:04:23,055
Okay.

90
00:04:23,097 --> 00:04:25,265
No! That's where I sit!

91
00:04:28,727 --> 00:04:31,188
What's the difference?

92
00:04:31,230 --> 00:04:33,272
This seat is ideally located

93
00:04:33,314 --> 00:04:35,650
both in relation to the
heat source in the winter

94
00:04:35,692 --> 00:04:37,569
and a cross breeze
in the summer.

95
00:04:37,611 --> 00:04:40,155
It also faces the television
at a direct angle--

96
00:04:40,197 --> 00:04:43,200
allowing me to immerse myself
in entertainment or game play

97
00:04:43,242 --> 00:04:44,952
without being subjected
to conversation.

98
00:04:44,993 --> 00:04:49,748
As a result, I've placed it
in a state of eternal dibs.

99
00:04:49,790 --> 00:04:52,042
Can you do that?

100
00:04:52,084 --> 00:04:54,753
Cathedra mea, regulae meae.

101
00:04:56,213 --> 00:04:59,340
That's Latin for
"my chair, my rules."

102
00:05:00,801 --> 00:05:03,637
Now, you said on the phone

103
00:05:03,678 --> 00:05:06,807
that your area of study
is physics.

104
00:05:06,848 --> 00:05:08,267
Uh, yeah, experimental physics.

105
00:05:08,308 --> 00:05:10,894
Hmm...

106
00:05:10,936 --> 00:05:12,771
What is that?

107
00:05:12,813 --> 00:05:13,981
Doesn't concern you.

108
00:05:14,022 --> 00:05:15,274
You'll be going

109
00:05:15,315 --> 00:05:17,734
to the university every day?
Yes.

110
00:05:17,776 --> 00:05:19,153
And you have a vehicle?
A car, yeah.

111
00:05:19,194 --> 00:05:20,320
And you'll be willing
to drive me?

112
00:05:20,361 --> 00:05:21,780
Can't you drive?

113
00:05:21,822 --> 00:05:23,531
I can.

114
00:05:25,575 --> 00:05:28,162
I choose not to.

115
00:05:28,203 --> 00:05:30,538
Okay, I suppose
I could drive you.

116
00:05:31,915 --> 00:05:33,959
That's a point in
my favor, right?

117
00:05:34,001 --> 00:05:36,377
Why don't you let
me do this.

118
00:05:36,419 --> 00:05:38,297
Come on, I just asked.

119
00:05:42,592 --> 00:05:43,677
Last question.

120
00:05:43,718 --> 00:05:45,304
In a post-apocalyptic world,

121
00:05:45,344 --> 00:05:48,140
which task would you assign
the highest priority?

122
00:05:48,182 --> 00:05:50,142
Locating a sustainable food
source,

123
00:05:50,184 --> 00:05:51,977
reestablishing a functioning
government,

124
00:05:52,019 --> 00:05:54,688
procreating, or preserving
the knowledge of mankind?

125
00:05:54,729 --> 00:05:58,192
Uh, I'm gonna go with...
preserving the knowledge.

126
00:05:58,233 --> 00:06:00,777
That's correct.

127
00:06:00,819 --> 00:06:04,614
FYI, I would have accepted any
answer other than procreating.

128
00:06:06,158 --> 00:06:08,118
Come, I'll show you
the rest of the apartment.

129
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:09,286
Oh, good.

130
00:06:09,328 --> 00:06:10,912
I passed the barriers.

131
00:06:10,954 --> 00:06:13,290
The second barrier.
Don't get cocky.

132
00:06:15,876 --> 00:06:18,670
This is the bathroom.

133
00:06:18,712 --> 00:06:20,214
Are you fairly regular?

134
00:06:22,381 --> 00:06:24,718
Uh, I guess.

135
00:06:24,759 --> 00:06:26,928
This isn't going to work
if you're guessing.

136
00:06:28,263 --> 00:06:30,682
When do you evacuate
your bowels?

137
00:06:30,724 --> 00:06:33,101
When I have to.

138
00:06:33,143 --> 00:06:34,728
When you have to?

139
00:06:34,769 --> 00:06:37,356
I'm sorry, I don't
rent to hippies.

140
00:06:40,317 --> 00:06:42,027
I-I'm sorry, uh,
in the morning.

141
00:06:42,069 --> 00:06:43,486
Around 8:00.

142
00:06:43,528 --> 00:06:47,448
I can't give you 8:00.
I can give you 7:30.

143
00:06:47,490 --> 00:06:49,284
Fine. I'll take it.

144
00:06:49,326 --> 00:06:52,329
Third barrier passed.

145
00:06:52,371 --> 00:06:54,539
You have won the right
to see your room.

146
00:06:54,581 --> 00:06:56,208
Huzzah.

147
00:06:59,169 --> 00:07:00,712
Is this it?

148
00:07:00,754 --> 00:07:02,130
No, this is my room.

149
00:07:02,172 --> 00:07:03,965
People don't go in my room.

150
00:07:04,007 --> 00:07:07,302
So where do you sleep?
(chuckles)

151
00:07:07,344 --> 00:07:09,179
I don't understand.

152
00:07:09,221 --> 00:07:12,724
If people don't go in there,
and you're people, and...

153
00:07:12,766 --> 00:07:15,394
You are people, aren't you?

154
00:07:16,395 --> 00:07:17,938
Making a joke.

155
00:07:17,979 --> 00:07:19,564
Do you do this often?

156
00:07:19,606 --> 00:07:22,276
On occasion.

157
00:07:26,445 --> 00:07:27,989
Your room.

158
00:07:35,914 --> 00:07:38,208
You may want to repaint.

159
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:43,213
And after all that,
you just moved in?

160
00:07:43,255 --> 00:07:44,423
I didn't just move in.

161
00:07:44,464 --> 00:07:46,591
First we had to iron out
a few details.

162
00:07:46,633 --> 00:07:50,053
"Roommates agree that Friday
nights shall be reserved

163
00:07:50,095 --> 00:07:51,346
"for watching Joss Whedon's

164
00:07:51,388 --> 00:07:53,807
brilliant new series
Firefly."

165
00:07:53,848 --> 00:07:57,018
Does that really need
to be in the agreement?

166
00:07:57,060 --> 00:08:00,897
We might as well settle it now
it's going to be on for years.

167
00:08:00,939 --> 00:08:03,024
Initial here.

168
00:08:04,443 --> 00:08:10,198
All right, that's
television and movies.

169
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:13,243
Section nine: miscellany.

170
00:08:13,285 --> 00:08:14,744
The apartment's flag

171
00:08:14,786 --> 00:08:18,999
is a gold lion rampant
on a field of azure.

172
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:20,583
We have a flag?

173
00:08:30,802 --> 00:08:32,346
Never fly it upside down.

174
00:08:33,805 --> 00:08:35,556
Unless the
apartment's in distress.

175
00:08:35,598 --> 00:08:37,100
And next.

176
00:08:37,142 --> 00:08:39,311
"If either of us ever
invents time travel,

177
00:08:39,353 --> 00:08:43,315
"we agree our first stop
will be this meeting today

178
00:08:43,357 --> 00:08:45,442
in precisely five seconds."

179
00:08:47,110 --> 00:08:49,029
Okay.

180
00:08:59,289 --> 00:09:01,582
That's disappointing.

181
00:09:03,335 --> 00:09:05,504
Why on earth did you
agree to all that?

182
00:09:05,545 --> 00:09:07,464
It was the best
apartment I'd seen,

183
00:09:07,506 --> 00:09:09,424
the rent was
very reasonable

184
00:09:09,466 --> 00:09:11,468
and after you've passed
the first three barriers

185
00:09:11,510 --> 00:09:13,845
you kind of want
to take it all the way.

186
00:09:13,887 --> 00:09:18,308
Well, I'm sorry, Leonard,
it's very hard to feel
sympathy for you.

187
00:09:18,350 --> 00:09:20,435
Okay, how about this? Let
me tell you about the first time

188
00:09:20,477 --> 00:09:21,560
I brought a girl over.

189
00:09:21,602 --> 00:09:23,480
(knocking)
SHELDON:
Leonard?

190
00:09:24,939 --> 00:09:27,608
Shh. Just pretend
we're not here.

191
00:09:29,069 --> 00:09:31,612
(louder knocking)
Leonard?

192
00:09:31,654 --> 00:09:33,823
I'm sure he'll go away.

193
00:09:35,909 --> 00:09:39,120
I'm just going to keep
knocking till you answer.

194
00:09:39,162 --> 00:09:40,205
(knocking)
Leonard?

195
00:09:40,247 --> 00:09:41,831
(knocking)
Leonard?

196
00:09:41,873 --> 00:09:42,749
(knocking)
Leonard?

197
00:09:42,790 --> 00:09:44,334
What do you want?!

198
00:09:44,376 --> 00:09:46,586
I didn't say come in!

199
00:09:46,627 --> 00:09:48,505
You asked what I wanted.

200
00:09:48,547 --> 00:09:49,964
I wanted to come in.

201
00:09:50,006 --> 00:09:51,799
I'm here

202
00:09:51,841 --> 00:09:53,968
because you violated our
roommate agreement.

203
00:09:54,010 --> 00:09:55,970
Specifically,
section eight--

204
00:09:56,012 --> 00:09:59,682
visitors, subsection C females,
paragraph four-- coitus.

205
00:09:59,724 --> 00:10:01,768
"Roommates shall
give each other

206
00:10:01,809 --> 00:10:05,188
12 hours notice
of impending coitus."

207
00:10:05,230 --> 00:10:08,358
I didn't even know
her 12 hours ago.

208
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:09,943
That's it!
I'm out of here!

209
00:10:09,984 --> 00:10:11,194
But, Joyce, come on.

210
00:10:11,236 --> 00:10:12,779
12 hours?

211
00:10:15,532 --> 00:10:17,158
Oh, my God.

212
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:18,868
Do I get some sympathy now?

213
00:10:18,910 --> 00:10:20,703
A little bit.

214
00:10:25,125 --> 00:10:26,709
Okay, let me get this straight.
You move in with this guy,

215
00:10:26,751 --> 00:10:29,670
he makes you sign a ridiculous
roommate agreement,

216
00:10:29,712 --> 00:10:31,672
then he walks
into your bedroom

217
00:10:31,714 --> 00:10:34,092
while you're doing this
Joyce Kim, and you still stay?

218
00:10:34,134 --> 00:10:36,719
Actually, I couldn't get too mad
at him about Joyce Kim.

219
00:10:36,761 --> 00:10:38,346
Why not?

220
00:10:38,388 --> 00:10:40,848
Well, I was doing some
government research at the time,

221
00:10:40,890 --> 00:10:42,601
you know, military,
rocket fuel.

222
00:10:42,641 --> 00:10:44,102
It's kind of secret.

223
00:10:44,144 --> 00:10:45,812
What does that have
to do with Joyce Kim?

224
00:10:45,853 --> 00:10:48,940
As it turns out,
she was a North Korean spy.

225
00:10:50,733 --> 00:10:52,735
Luckily, Sheldon drove her out

226
00:10:52,777 --> 00:10:54,737
before I revealed
anything important.

227
00:10:54,779 --> 00:10:57,115
Which I-I'm not saying
I would have.

228
00:10:58,074 --> 00:10:59,534
So, what, that's it?

229
00:10:59,576 --> 00:11:01,244
You've stayed with
Sheldon all this time

230
00:11:01,286 --> 00:11:03,163
because he kept you from
going to federal prison?

231
00:11:03,204 --> 00:11:04,705
That was part of it.

232
00:11:04,747 --> 00:11:07,083
The other part is what happened
with the elevator.

233
00:11:07,125 --> 00:11:08,543
Oh, yeah, I'm
wondering about that.

234
00:11:08,585 --> 00:11:10,587
You said it was working
when you moved in.

235
00:11:10,629 --> 00:11:13,548
It was, but one night,
Sheldon came home from work...

236
00:11:13,590 --> 00:11:15,634
(video game music
and sound effects blaring)

237
00:11:23,016 --> 00:11:25,726
What is going on here?

238
00:11:25,768 --> 00:11:26,769
Hey, Sheldon.

239
00:11:26,811 --> 00:11:27,895
This is Howard and Raj.

240
00:11:27,937 --> 00:11:29,272
They work at the
university, too.

241
00:11:29,314 --> 00:11:30,524
Hey.
Hey.

242
00:11:30,565 --> 00:11:31,650
I'll get to you later.

243
00:11:32,775 --> 00:11:34,735
What are you sitting on?

244
00:11:34,777 --> 00:11:38,072
I can't speak for these guys,
but I'm sitting on my tushie.

245
00:11:39,658 --> 00:11:40,699
It's a joke.

246
00:11:40,741 --> 00:11:42,410
Not a good idea.

247
00:11:42,452 --> 00:11:44,371
"Tushie"
is buttocks, right?

248
00:11:44,412 --> 00:11:47,374
Right.
(laughing):
Hilarious.

249
00:11:47,415 --> 00:11:49,334
Explain the couch.

250
00:11:49,376 --> 00:11:50,544
Oh, well, there
were some people

251
00:11:50,585 --> 00:11:51,710
on the first floor
moving out,

252
00:11:51,752 --> 00:11:53,129
and they sold it
to me for $100.

253
00:11:53,171 --> 00:11:54,755
Howard and Raj helped
me bring it up.

254
00:11:54,797 --> 00:11:57,716
But what's wrong
with the furniture we have?

255
00:11:57,758 --> 00:12:00,845
They're lawn chairs.

256
00:12:00,887 --> 00:12:02,721
And there was no place
for company.

257
00:12:02,763 --> 00:12:06,309
Did it occur to you
that was by design?

258
00:12:06,351 --> 00:12:07,686
According
to the roommate agreement,

259
00:12:07,726 --> 00:12:09,688
I'm entitled to allocate 50%

260
00:12:09,728 --> 00:12:11,690
of the cubic footage
of the common areas.

261
00:12:11,730 --> 00:12:13,692
But you didn't notify me
by e-mail,

262
00:12:13,732 --> 00:12:15,985
so this is still a breach.

263
00:12:16,027 --> 00:12:17,778
I did notify you.

264
00:12:17,820 --> 00:12:20,114
Oh, you did, did you?

265
00:12:35,004 --> 00:12:36,715
Oh, drat!

266
00:12:38,174 --> 00:12:41,177
Hoisted by my own spam filter.

267
00:12:41,219 --> 00:12:43,179
What am I doing
in your spam folder?

268
00:12:43,221 --> 00:12:45,014
I put you there
after you forwarded me

269
00:12:45,056 --> 00:12:46,600
a picture of a cat
playing the piano,

270
00:12:46,641 --> 00:12:48,059
entitled "This is funny."

271
00:12:49,102 --> 00:12:50,520
Oh, yeah, I saw that.

272
00:12:50,562 --> 00:12:52,939
That was hilarious.

273
00:12:52,980 --> 00:12:54,733
Okay, what does all this have
to do with the elevator?

274
00:12:54,773 --> 00:12:56,192
I'm getting to it.

275
00:12:56,234 --> 00:12:58,069
I assure you,
you'll be sorry

276
00:12:58,111 --> 00:13:00,196
you wasted your money
on an iPod

277
00:13:00,238 --> 00:13:03,032
when Microsoft comes
out with theirs.

278
00:13:04,909 --> 00:13:07,245
Okay, do you have an opinion
about everything?

279
00:13:07,287 --> 00:13:10,331
Yes.

280
00:13:10,373 --> 00:13:12,542
And you just assume
you're always right?

281
00:13:12,584 --> 00:13:15,086
It's not an assumption.

282
00:13:16,087 --> 00:13:17,547
Change seats with me.

283
00:13:17,589 --> 00:13:19,549
Why?
I don't like this spot.

284
00:13:19,591 --> 00:13:21,217
I have to keep turning my head.

285
00:13:21,259 --> 00:13:22,927
Fine.

286
00:13:24,178 --> 00:13:26,264
Ooh, it's time for Babylon 5!

287
00:13:26,306 --> 00:13:28,683
We don't watch Babylon 5
in this apartment.

288
00:13:28,725 --> 00:13:29,892
Why not?

289
00:13:29,934 --> 00:13:31,978
Because no one
likes Babylon 5.

290
00:13:32,019 --> 00:13:33,020
I like it.

291
00:13:33,062 --> 00:13:34,272
Me, too.
So do I.

292
00:13:34,314 --> 00:13:35,690
There you go--
three against one.

293
00:13:35,732 --> 00:13:37,024
They don't get a vote.

294
00:13:37,066 --> 00:13:38,443
It's one against one.

295
00:13:38,485 --> 00:13:40,153
And according
to the roommate agreement,

296
00:13:40,194 --> 00:13:43,030
all ties will be settled by me.

297
00:13:43,072 --> 00:13:45,283
But I said no to that.

298
00:13:45,325 --> 00:13:47,327
And I said yes.

299
00:13:47,368 --> 00:13:49,996
And I settle all ties.

300
00:13:53,625 --> 00:13:55,084
Change seats with me.

301
00:13:55,126 --> 00:13:56,461
Why?

302
00:13:56,503 --> 00:13:58,087
There's a draft
on my neck over here.

303
00:13:58,129 --> 00:13:59,714
So, I get the draft?

304
00:13:59,756 --> 00:14:02,091
You're protected
by your turtleneck.

305
00:14:03,134 --> 00:14:04,511
Fine.

306
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:08,306
And it's a dickey.

307
00:14:15,396 --> 00:14:17,940
Hmm...

308
00:14:17,982 --> 00:14:20,234
I'm still not comfortable.

309
00:14:20,276 --> 00:14:21,361
Of course.

310
00:14:21,402 --> 00:14:23,029
There's too many people here.

311
00:14:24,572 --> 00:14:25,906
We can fix that.

312
00:14:25,948 --> 00:14:27,784
Let's leave.

313
00:14:27,826 --> 00:14:30,203
Yeah, we can go
over to my place.

314
00:14:30,244 --> 00:14:31,287
Wait.

315
00:14:31,329 --> 00:14:33,247
Let me get my jacket.

316
00:14:34,624 --> 00:14:36,959
You're not going with us.
Why not?

317
00:14:37,001 --> 00:14:40,004
You're the guy we're
trying to get away from.

318
00:14:40,046 --> 00:14:41,589
Oh.

319
00:14:41,631 --> 00:14:43,882
Well, in that case,
I don't need my jacket.

320
00:14:45,468 --> 00:14:47,220
And for the record,
the correct syntax is:

321
00:14:47,261 --> 00:14:49,972
I'm the guy from whom
you're trying to get away.

322
00:14:56,396 --> 00:14:58,897
(door closes)

323
00:14:58,939 --> 00:15:01,442
Oh, yes.

324
00:15:04,320 --> 00:15:07,114
This is definitely
going to be my spot.

325
00:15:07,156 --> 00:15:08,700
PENNY:
Okay.

326
00:15:08,742 --> 00:15:10,660
How do you know he said that?
You left the room.

327
00:15:10,702 --> 00:15:14,288
Hey, do you want me to finish
working on your man feet or not?

328
00:15:15,331 --> 00:15:17,876
Fine. Go ahead.

329
00:15:17,916 --> 00:15:21,087
MRS. WOLOWITZ (shouting):
Howard, are you having
a playdate?

330
00:15:21,128 --> 00:15:23,297
I don't have playdates!

331
00:15:23,339 --> 00:15:25,091
I have colleagues!

332
00:15:25,132 --> 00:15:28,845
Do their parents
know they're here?

333
00:15:28,887 --> 00:15:32,515
No, but if you keep screaming,
maybe they'll hear you!

334
00:15:37,061 --> 00:15:38,521
That your dad?

335
00:15:40,815 --> 00:15:43,693
She grows any more hair
on her face, yes.

336
00:15:45,027 --> 00:15:46,237
Oh, man.

337
00:15:46,279 --> 00:15:48,281
Is that a two-stage rocket?

338
00:15:48,322 --> 00:15:50,909
Three. I designed
the engine myself.

339
00:15:50,949 --> 00:15:53,411
Cool. Can it break Mach 1?

340
00:15:53,453 --> 00:15:56,289
Oh, probably, if I could get
my hands on that new fuel

341
00:15:56,330 --> 00:15:58,332
the government's
been working on.

342
00:15:58,374 --> 00:16:00,334
Oh, this just might be
your lucky day.

343
00:16:00,376 --> 00:16:02,503
MRS. WOLOWITZ:
Howard, what happened
to the Oreos

344
00:16:02,545 --> 00:16:04,798
I left on the counter?!

345
00:16:06,466 --> 00:16:09,427
I haven't seen your Oreos!

346
00:16:09,469 --> 00:16:12,931
Just take your bath
without them!

347
00:16:16,935 --> 00:16:18,227
So, why was it
his lucky day?

348
00:16:18,269 --> 00:16:19,437
Well, it turns out I had

349
00:16:19,479 --> 00:16:21,063
a little rocket fuel
in the apartment.

350
00:16:21,105 --> 00:16:23,399
What were you doing with
rocket fuel in your apartment?

351
00:16:23,441 --> 00:16:25,276
Mm, Joyce Kim
was kind of curious

352
00:16:25,318 --> 00:16:26,778
about what I did
for a living,

353
00:16:26,820 --> 00:16:29,614
and I was going to kind of
show it to her.

354
00:16:31,324 --> 00:16:32,575
It's not important.
The point is,

355
00:16:32,617 --> 00:16:34,619
the guys and I went back
to the apartment.

356
00:16:34,661 --> 00:16:36,120
Are we ever going to
get to the elevator?

357
00:16:36,162 --> 00:16:37,288
Yeah, we're really close.

358
00:16:37,330 --> 00:16:38,581
Uh, uh, we're at
the apartment.

359
00:16:38,623 --> 00:16:40,667
The trick is to
mix it into Tovex

360
00:16:40,708 --> 00:16:42,460
in order to create
a combustible gel

361
00:16:42,502 --> 00:16:45,546
that will generate over 8,000
kilonewtons of thrust.

362
00:16:45,588 --> 00:16:46,631
(both giggling)
Cool.
Nice.

363
00:16:46,673 --> 00:16:49,342
SHELDON:
Won't work.

364
00:16:49,383 --> 00:16:51,970
Excuse me, but I've been working
on this a long time.

365
00:16:52,011 --> 00:16:53,429
Trust me--
it'll work.

366
00:16:53,471 --> 00:16:55,515
You don't see your mistake,
do you?

367
00:16:55,556 --> 00:16:57,057
There's no mistake.

368
00:16:57,099 --> 00:16:59,769
This is for a full-scale
rocket, not a model.

369
00:16:59,811 --> 00:17:01,521
Well, I've adjusted
the formula.

370
00:17:01,562 --> 00:17:03,564
Not correctly.

371
00:17:03,606 --> 00:17:05,566
Okay, I've had it with you.

372
00:17:05,608 --> 00:17:07,610
You might be an expert

373
00:17:07,652 --> 00:17:09,403
on theoretical physics
and science-fiction programs

374
00:17:09,445 --> 00:17:11,489
and where to sit
on a freaking couch,

375
00:17:11,531 --> 00:17:13,574
but this is applied physics.

376
00:17:13,616 --> 00:17:16,911
And when it comes
to applied physic... uh-oh.

377
00:17:16,953 --> 00:17:18,204
What's happening?

378
00:17:18,245 --> 00:17:19,956
A bad thing. A very bad thing.

379
00:17:19,998 --> 00:17:21,123
Get the door.

380
00:17:21,165 --> 00:17:22,416
Get the door!
Get the door!

381
00:17:22,458 --> 00:17:24,669
Get the door!
Get the door!

382
00:17:34,637 --> 00:17:37,015
You're waiting
for the elevator?!

383
00:17:37,056 --> 00:17:38,808
Oh. Right.

384
00:17:38,850 --> 00:17:40,518
(elevator bell dings)
Wait. It's here.

385
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:42,020
(groans)

386
00:17:44,898 --> 00:17:46,566
Give me that.

387
00:17:50,069 --> 00:17:51,738
What'd you do that for?
I had plenty of time...

388
00:17:51,779 --> 00:17:54,490
(explosion)

389
00:17:57,201 --> 00:17:58,995
You're welcome.

390
00:18:02,081 --> 00:18:03,833
Not only did
Sheldon save my life,

391
00:18:03,875 --> 00:18:05,626
he didn't rat me out
to the landlord.

392
00:18:05,668 --> 00:18:06,836
Or the police.

393
00:18:06,878 --> 00:18:09,213
Or Homeland Security.

394
00:18:09,255 --> 00:18:11,257
Okay, so, basically,
you're the reason

395
00:18:11,298 --> 00:18:13,593
I have to walk up and
down three flights of
stairs every day?

396
00:18:13,634 --> 00:18:15,177
So I did something stupid.

397
00:18:15,219 --> 00:18:17,263
I'm sure you did stupid things
when you were younger.

398
00:18:17,304 --> 00:18:18,723
What were you doing
seven years ago?

399
00:18:18,765 --> 00:18:20,558
Excuse me, I was
in high school.

400
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:22,560
Studying, keeping
my nose clean,

401
00:18:22,602 --> 00:18:24,478
doing volunteer work
for the community.

402
00:18:27,065 --> 00:18:29,442
Not pregnant.

403
00:18:29,483 --> 00:18:31,652
Yes!

404
00:18:31,694 --> 00:18:33,696
(laughs)

405
00:18:35,740 --> 00:18:38,076
Oh, Ubuntu,

406
00:18:38,117 --> 00:18:41,996
you are my favorite Linux-based
operating system.

407
00:18:42,038 --> 00:18:43,205
Hey.

408
00:18:43,247 --> 00:18:45,207
Hello.

409
00:18:45,249 --> 00:18:46,542
(sniffing)

410
00:18:46,584 --> 00:18:48,502
Why do I smell methacrylate?

411
00:18:48,544 --> 00:18:50,088
Oh, uh, clear nail polish.

412
00:18:50,129 --> 00:18:51,631
I had a mani-pedi.

413
00:18:52,548 --> 00:18:55,551
Men can get those.

414
00:18:55,593 --> 00:18:57,095
Anyway, I may owe you
an apology.

415
00:18:57,136 --> 00:19:00,514
There's doubt?

416
00:19:00,556 --> 00:19:02,100
I did agree
to the thermostat setting,

417
00:19:02,140 --> 00:19:04,143
and I shouldn't have tried
to change it.

418
00:19:04,184 --> 00:19:06,145
That's not an apology.

419
00:19:06,186 --> 00:19:09,189
Simply an acknowledgement
that I was right.

420
00:19:09,231 --> 00:19:10,566
Okay, I'm sorry.

421
00:19:10,608 --> 00:19:12,318
There you go.

422
00:19:12,359 --> 00:19:13,569
So, we're good?

423
00:19:13,611 --> 00:19:16,405
Good what?

424
00:19:16,447 --> 00:19:18,116
Never mind.

425
00:19:18,157 --> 00:19:20,118
Okay if I watch some TV?

426
00:19:20,158 --> 00:19:21,995
Go ahead.

427
00:19:22,036 --> 00:19:24,038
ANNOUNCER:
Up next Babylon 5.

428
00:19:25,957 --> 00:19:27,792
LEONARD:
You're not even
watching!

429
00:19:27,834 --> 00:19:29,209
SHELDON:
I can hear it!

430
00:19:29,251 --> 00:19:31,211
LEONARD:
Oh, so the dialogue
offends you?

431
00:19:31,253 --> 00:19:32,964
SHELDON:
I would hardly call
that dialogue!

432
00:19:33,006 --> 00:19:34,757
LEONARD:
You're insane, you
know that?!

433
00:19:34,799 --> 00:19:36,550
SHELDON:
Don't make me turn that
flag upside down,

434
00:19:36,592 --> 00:19:39,595
'cause you know
I'll do it!

